* Long-channel limit of BSIM3: every second-order effect the model provides a
* switch for, switched off. What remains is the textbook square law, so the
* parameters the card DECLARES can be recovered from the engine's own transfer
* curve and compared against the numbers the card actually produces.
*
* PCLM cannot be set to 0 -- BSIM3's parameter check refuses it outright
* ("Fatal: Pclm = 0 is not positive") -- so a negligible positive value is the
* only way to switch channel-length modulation off.
.model n1 nmos level=8 vth0=0.5 u0=600 tox=9n
+ dvt0=0 dvt1=0 dvt2=0 dvt0w=0 dvt1w=0 dvt2w=0
+ k3=0 w0=0 nlx=0 eta0=0 etab=0
+ pdiblc1=0 pdiblc2=0 pdiblcb=0 pscbe1=1e12 pscbe2=0
+ rdsw=0 prwg=0 prwb=0 wr=0
+ ua=0 ub=0 uc=0 vsat=1e9
+ k1=0 k2=0 cit=0 cdsc=0 cdscb=0 cdscd=0
+ nfactor=1 noff=1 voff=0 voffcv=0
+ delta=0 lint=0 xl=0
+ pclm=1e-12
